1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,590
You tap play on a video.

2
00:00:01,590 --> 00:00:13,660
In under a second, your request leaves your phone, crosses your Wi Fi router, your internet provider, a fibre backbone and a data centre, and thousands of packets of video come streaming back.

3
00:00:13,867 --> 00:00:16,303
Every step follows agreed rules.

4
00:00:16,303 --> 00:00:22,063
This unit explains the media, the devices, the layers and the addresses that make it work.

5
00:00:27,533 --> 00:00:33,251
Welcome to Class 12 Computer Science, Unit 2: Data Communication and Networking.

6
00:00:35,633 --> 00:00:36,872
Seven parts.

7
00:00:36,872 --> 00:00:38,564
The communication system.

8
00:00:38,564 --> 00:00:41,989
Signals, transmission modes and media.

9
00:00:41,989 --> 00:00:43,812
Transmission impairments.

10
00:00:43,812 --> 00:00:46,924
Network types, architectures and devices.

11
00:00:46,924 --> 00:00:48,226
Topologies.

12
00:00:48,226 --> 00:00:50,218
The OSI reference model.

13
00:00:50,218 --> 00:00:52,248
And IP addressing and subnetting.

14
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:08,359
Every communication needs five things: a message, a sender, a receiver, a medium for it to travel through, and a protocol, the agreed set of rules.

15
00:01:08,359 --> 00:01:15,999
Without a shared protocol, a perfect connection is useless, like two people with no common language on a working phone line.

16
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:19,558
Examiners ask for this diagram.

17
00:01:19,558 --> 00:01:33,885
The source produces the data; the transmitter turns it into a signal the medium can carry; the transmission system carries it, and unavoidably adds noise; the receiver turns the signal back into data; and the destination uses it.

18
00:01:38,667 --> 00:01:41,611
A signal represents data physically.

19
00:01:41,611 --> 00:01:45,856
An analogue signal varies continuously, like a voice.

20
00:01:45,856 --> 00:01:49,749
A digital signal jumps between levels for nought and one.

21
00:01:49,749 --> 00:01:56,216
Noise on an analogue signal piles up for good, while a digital signal can be regenerated perfectly.

22
00:01:56,433 --> 00:01:59,403
Bandwidth is how much a channel can carry.

23
00:01:59,403 --> 00:02:04,781
Bit rate counts bits per second; baud rate counts signal changes per second.

24
00:02:04,781 --> 00:02:10,834
If each change carries several bits, the bit rate is the baud rate times the bits per change.

25
00:02:12,067 --> 00:02:13,501
Transmission modes.

26
00:02:13,501 --> 00:02:17,121
Simplex goes one way only, like television.

27
00:02:17,121 --> 00:02:23,045
Half duplex goes both ways, but one at a time, like a walkie talkie: say over.

28
00:02:23,045 --> 00:02:26,249
Full duplex goes both ways at once, like a phone call.

29
00:02:27,467 --> 00:02:30,542
Guided media carry the signal along a cable.

30
00:02:30,542 --> 00:02:37,859
Twisted pair, the cheapest, twists its wires so that interference cancels out: most school networks use it.

31
00:02:37,859 --> 00:02:43,914
Coaxial cable has a central core and a braided shield, used for cable television.

32
00:02:43,914 --> 00:02:54,955
Optical fibre carries light, trapped in the glass core by total internal reflection: enormous bandwidth, long distances, and immune to electrical interference.

33
00:02:56,167 --> 00:02:59,814
Unguided media send signals through free space.

34
00:02:59,814 --> 00:03:04,398
Radio waves spread in all directions and pass through walls.

35
00:03:04,398 --> 00:03:07,952
Microwaves need line of sight; Wi Fi uses them.

36
00:03:07,952 --> 00:03:10,452
Infrared works only within a room.

37
00:03:10,452 --> 00:03:15,778
Satellites cover the globe, but with a delay of about a quarter of a second each way.

38
00:03:15,778 --> 00:03:21,014
Wireless is quick to install and mobile, but less secure: anyone in range can listen.

39
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:29,109
A signal never arrives exactly as it was sent.

40
00:03:29,109 --> 00:03:34,135
Attenuation: it weakens with distance, so repeaters regenerate it.

41
00:03:34,135 --> 00:03:40,033
Distortion: its shape changes, because different frequencies travel at different speeds.

42
00:03:40,033 --> 00:03:49,987
And noise: unwanted energy added on the way, from heat in the wires, from neighbouring wires, which is crosstalk, or from sudden spikes like lightning.

43
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:52,517
More impairments.

44
00:03:52,517 --> 00:03:55,342
Crosstalk: you faintly hear another call.

45
00:03:55,342 --> 00:03:57,751
Echo: you hear yourself, delayed.

46
00:03:57,751 --> 00:04:02,842
Jitter: packets arriving unevenly, which ruins a video call.

47
00:04:02,842 --> 00:04:09,462
And bandwidth itself is a limit: a channel simply cannot carry changes faster than its bandwidth.

48
00:04:14,233 --> 00:04:16,852
Networks are named by the area they cover.

49
00:04:16,852 --> 00:04:23,115
A local area network covers a room, a building or a campus: fast and private.

50
00:04:23,115 --> 00:04:25,836
A metropolitan network covers a city.

51
00:04:25,836 --> 00:04:31,643
A wide area network spans a country or the world, the Internet being the biggest.

52
00:04:31,643 --> 00:04:34,962
And a personal area network covers just a few metres.

53
00:04:36,167 --> 00:04:37,588
Two architectures.

54
00:04:37,588 --> 00:04:49,411
In client server, a dedicated server provides services and clients request them: one place for accounts, security and backups, but if the server dies, everyone stops.

55
00:04:49,411 --> 00:04:59,106
In peer to peer, every computer is both client and server: cheap and simple for a small office, but security depends on every user.

56
00:05:00,333 --> 00:05:01,767
Network devices.

57
00:05:01,767 --> 00:05:10,165
A hub is a dumb repeater: a frame from A to D is copied to every port, wasting bandwidth and causing collisions.

58
00:05:10,165 --> 00:05:15,522
A switch learns which MAC address sits on which port and sends the frame only to D.

59
00:05:16,733 --> 00:05:19,560
The full list, with each device's layer.

60
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:21,774
A network card connects a computer.

61
00:05:21,774 --> 00:05:25,068
A modem converts between digital and analogue.

62
00:05:25,068 --> 00:05:28,310
Repeaters and hubs work on raw signals.

63
00:05:28,310 --> 00:05:31,578
Bridges and switches forward by MAC address.

64
00:05:31,578 --> 00:05:36,500
Routers join different networks and choose paths by IP address.

65
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:39,950
And a gateway translates between different protocols.

